sangati rashi

  -- rashi: a quantity instance — the particular, situated thing being measured.
  -- distinct from the kosha concept (velocity, mass) which is the universal/type.
  -- the kosha concept is atemporal; rashi is situated in a problem context.
  --
  -- a rashi has three aspects:
  --   vishesa  — the type  (which quantity: velocity, mass, force, ...)
  --   sankhya  — the magnitude (how much) — optional: may be unknown or symbolic
  --   matra    — the unit  (in what terms: metre-per-second, kilogram, ...)
  --
  -- rashi instances: v1, v2, m1, m2, a, F are all rashi
  --   [v1, vishesa, velocity]          -- v1 IS a velocity
  --   [v1, vishesa, rashi]             -- v1 is a measurable quantity (general)
  --   [v1, sankhya, "20.0"]            -- v1's magnitude is 20.0  (if known)
  --   [v1, matra,   metre-per-second]  -- v1 is measured in m/s
  --
  -- a rashi WITHOUT sankhya is a symbolic quantity:
  --   v1 and v2 in "compare v1 and v2" are both rashi with vishesa→velocity
  --   but neither has a sankhya — they are instances, not yet measured
  --   this is valid and meaningful: the graph can reason about their relation
  --   without knowing their numeric values
  --
  -- sankhya is not the quantity — it is what a quantity carries when instantiated.
  -- the quantity is rashi. sankhya is what rashi optionally holds.

  "pramana-swarupa"
  "vishesa-sthita"        -- typed by a kosha concept
  "sankhya-yukta"         -- optionally carries a numeric magnitude
  "matra-yukta"           -- measured in a unit
  "sambandha-yukta"       -- related to other rashi in a problem
  "prashna-sthita"        -- arises in question context
  "viveka-kriya"          -- viveka resolves which kosha concept it instantiates

done
